WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Course structure is one of the administrator's strongest governance tools.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:15.480
Categories are not just folders for tidiness; they influence navigation, delegated management, reporting, enrolment patterns, archival decisions, and support ownership.

3
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:26.940
A category structure based only on today's visual preference may become difficult to operate when programs change, reporting requirements appear, or different teams need limited administrative authority.

4
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.380
Course defaults should reduce repetitive setup without hiding decisions.

5
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:46.280
Visibility, format, completion tracking, enrolment methods, group behavior, file limits, naming patterns, start and end dates, and backup expectations all shape the way teachers and managers experience the site.

6
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:53.900
A default that fits a short compliance course may not fit a semester program, professional development track, or open self-paced resource.

7
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.200
Administrators should document what the defaults assume.

8
00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.880
Course creation needs a lifecycle.

9
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.500
Who may request a course?

10
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.800
Who approves it?

11
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.180
Who owns the content?

12
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.960
Which category should contain it?

13
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.900
Which naming standard applies?

14
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.160
Which enrolment method is expected?

15
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.460
Who supports learners?

16
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.120
When does the course open, close, archive, or get reused?

17
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:26.780
Without answers, the site gradually fills with duplicate courses, stale content, unclear ownership, and reports that require manual explanation.

18
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:34.260
Moodle course copy, import, backup, and restore are related but not the same operation.

19
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.200
Copying a course can reproduce structure for reuse.

20
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.620
Import can bring selected activities or resources from another course.

21
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.720
Backup creates a package that may include different sets of content and data depending on choices.

22
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.580
Restore creates or updates a course from a backup package.

23
00:01:52.680 --> 00:02:00.280
Administrators should be precise because each action has different privacy, data-retention, and operational consequences.

24
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.360
Completion settings and grade-related configurations also need governance.

25
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.300
Course completion can record that defined conditions were met, but it is not automatic proof of competence.

26
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:19.160
The organization decides what completion means, what evidence is sufficient, who may change settings, and how exceptions are handled.

27
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:25.420
Logs and completion reports can support review, but they do not replace assessment design or human judgment.

28
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.700
A sustainable Moodle course estate has fewer mysteries.

29
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:42.040
Category owners are known, course creators have scoped rights, templates are maintained, enrolment methods are intentional, archived courses are handled consistently, and reuse decisions preserve privacy.

30
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:51.900
Good structure makes support easier because administrators can tell whether a problem belongs to the course design, the category policy, the enrolment source, or the platform baseline.
